<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haojing</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haojing, or Hao, was the main capital of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>, founded by King Wu on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-river">Feng River</a> south-west of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fengjing">Fengjing</a> on the opposite bank it formed the Zhou royal centre, known as Zongzhou. In 771 BCE the Marquis of Shen and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanrong">Quanrong</a> sacked it and killed King You, and the court moved east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengzhou">Luoyi</a>, beginning the Eastern Zhou.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Haojing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fengjing">Fengjing</a> stood on opposite banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-river">Feng River</a>, Feng on the west bank and Hao on the east, and together the two settlements were known as Fenghao, the capital of the Western Zhou dynasty (c. 1046 – c. 771 BCE). Archaeological discoveries indicate that the ruins of Haojing lie next to the Feng River, around the north end of Doumen Subdistrict in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>, Shaanxi Province.</p><p>The two halves had different roles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fengjing">Fengjing</a> became the site of the Zhou ancestral shrine and gardens, while Haojing contained the royal residence and the seat of government. The name Zongzhou marked its role as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states, most of them ruled by clans claiming collateral descent from the royal Ji family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ji Chang, posthumously honoured as King Wen, moved the predynastic Zhou capital about 100 kilometres east from Qiyi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fengjing">Fengjing</a> in the mid-11th century BCE. Traditional accounts place the move five years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muye">Battle of Muye</a>, which present scholarship dates to c. 1046 BCE. After his son Fa, King Wu, defeated the Shang and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>, he moved his court across the river to the shore of Lake Hao.</p><p>During the reign of King Cheng (1042–1021 BCE) the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a> built a second settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengzhou">Luoyi</a>, also known as Chengzhou, to reinforce control of the eastern part of the kingdom. Although King Cheng was permanently stationed there, Haojing remained the main centre of operations until the time of King Zhao (r. 996–977 BCE), when Chengzhou took over that role.</p><p>In the reign of King You (r. 781–771 BCE) the Marquess of Shen overran Haojing with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanrong">Quanrong</a> nomads from the west, ending the Western Zhou. All the royal buildings of Haojing were razed to the ground, although it is not known whether those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fengjing">Fengjing</a> were destroyed as well. The newly enthroned King Ping (r. 770–720 BCE) then formally moved the capital east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengzhou">Chengzhou</a>. For some time afterwards there were still people who called themselves Western Zhou and gave the name Zongzhou to Chengzhou.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haojing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The main royal capital remained <b>Haojing</b>, founded by King Wu beside Feng near modern Xi'an.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Quanrong sacked <b>Haojing</b> in 771 BCE, King Ping moved the court east to Luoyi (Luoyang), under the protection of the State of Jin and the State of Zheng, beginning the Eastern Zhou.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-zhou">Eastern Zhou</a></li><li><blockquote>When King You demoted his queen, a daughter of the Marquis of Shen, in favour of Bao Si, the Marquis allied with the Quanrong and the State of Zeng and sacked <b>Haojing</b> in 771 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-shen">State of Shen</a></li></ul></section>
